2026-11-19 20:17:01 - Agent_20261119201419 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 4 results of the "dangerous sinks from parameters" (CWE-94/CWE-502) query in pydantic.

1. pydantic/v1/parse.py:42 — `pickle.loads(bb)` in `load_str_bytes`. Reached only when `proto == Protocol.pickle`, which requires either an explicit non-default `proto` argument supplied in code or `allow_pickle=True` plus a `*pickle` content type. Guarded by `if not allow_pickle: raise RuntimeError(...)`, default `allow_pickle=False`. This is an intentional, documented escape hatch (parse_raw/parse_file with explicit opt-in), not a vulnerability reachable from untrusted input by default. → FP.

2. pydantic/deprecated/parse.py:54 — identical `pickle.loads(bb)` logic in the deprecated v2 shim; same `allow_pickle=False` default and explicit RuntimeError guard, and additionally decorated `@deprecated`. → FP.

3. pydantic/_internal/_validators.py:87 — `import_module(module_path)` inside `_import_string_logic`. This is the implementation of the public `ImportString`/`PyObject` type (used via `core_schema.no_info_plain_validator_function(function=_validators.import_string)` in pydantic/types.py:459-466). Importing a dotted path supplied by the user is the entire, documented purpose of the `ImportString` annotation; a model author must explicitly declare the field type to opt in. No attacker-controlled path reaches this sink from any pydantic-internal default flow, and `import_module` + `getattr` does not execute attacker-chosen callables (only returns the object; attribute access is constrained to a single name). → FP.

4. pydantic/v1/utils.py:133 (`import_string`) — same pattern as #3 for the v1 `PyObject` type (called from pydantic/v1/types.py:657 after `str_validator`). Documented Django-derived helper used only when a field is annotated `PyObject`; not reachable from any implicit parsing path. → FP.

Also checked callers: pydantic/main.py parse_raw/parse_file and pydantic/v1/main.py parse_raw/parse_file, pydantic/v1/tools.py parse_obj_as/parse_file_as — all forward `allow_pickle` defaults of False and require explicit opt-in. pydantic/_migration.py `import_string` calls use constant lookup tables (MOVED_IN_V2 / DEPRECATED_MOVED_IN_V2 / REDIRECT_TO_V1), not user input.

No new exploitable vulnerabilities discovered. All four are by-design, opt-in APIs with safe defaults; classifying as false positives.
